### Which @angular/* package(s) are the source of the bug?

core

### Is this a regression?

No

### Description

Destroying a `resource()` calls `AbortController.abort()` without a reason. Per the DOM Standard that creates a new `"AbortError"` `DOMException` for every destroyed resource, and in Chromium creating it captures a JavaScript stack trace whose cost grows with the depth of the call stack. Resources are destroyed inside the view destroy recursion, where the stack is deep, so this adds up when a page with many resources is torn down.

**Where it happens** (`main` at e0260db):

- `ResourceImpl` creates an `AbortController` per load and keeps it after the load has resolved: [resource.ts#L442](https://github.com/angular/angular/blob/e0260db54c25529205901a20cd33d72c974061f0/packages/core/src/resource/resource.ts#L442)
- `destroy()` calls `abortInProgressLoad()`: [resource.ts#L397-L401](https://github.com/angular/angular/blob/e0260db54c25529205901a20cd33d72c974061f0/packages/core/src/resource/resource.ts#L397-L401)
- `abortInProgressLoad()` aborts without a reason: [resource.ts#L519-L520](https://github.com/angular/angular/blob/e0260db54c25529205901a20cd33d72c974061f0/packages/core/src/resource/resource.ts#L519-L520)
- `rxResource` relies on this abort to unsubscribe its Observable: [rx_resource.ts#L89-L100](https://github.com/angular/angular/blob/e0260db54c25529205901a20cd33d72c974061f0/packages/core/rxjs-interop/src/rx_resource.ts#L89-L100)

So every resource that has loaded once calls `abort()` when it is destroyed.

**What the spec says:**

- [`abort(reason)`](https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#dom-abortcontroller-abort): "The abort(reason) method steps are to signal abort on this with reason if it is given."
- [signal abort](https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#abortsignal-signal-abort), step 2: "Set signal's abort reason to reason if it is given; otherwise to a new "AbortError" DOMException."

**Measurements** (Chromium 153 via Playwright 1.63, 4× CPU throttling, default `Error.stackTraceLimit` of 10), cost per call:

| Call stack depth | `abort()` | `abort(reason)` |
|---|---|---|
| 5 | 14 µs | 0.8 µs |
| 100 | 50 µs | 1.8 µs |
| 300 | 150 µs | 1.0 µs |

In our application, navigating away from a page destroys 88–95 resources. Their `abort()` calls take 24–35 ms per navigation (4× throttling, about 300 µs per call inside the destroy recursion). With a shared reason (see the workaround below) they take 5–10 ms; the rest is the abort listeners themselves (unsubscribing).

**Possible fix:** pass a reason, for example one `DOMException('...', 'AbortError')` created once at module level. `ResourceImpl` itself only checks `abortSignal.aborted`, and loaders that forward the signal (e.g. to `fetch`) still see an `AbortError` as the reason, so the observable behaviour stays the same apart from the shared instance.

**Workaround** we use until then, in the application bootstrap:

```ts
const sharedAbortReason = new DOMException('signal is aborted without reason', 'AbortError');
const nativeAbort = AbortController.prototype.abort;
AbortController.prototype.abort = function (reason?: unknown) {
  nativeAbort.call(this, reason === undefined ? sharedAbortReason : reason);
};
```

### Please provide a link to a minimal reproduction of the bug

No StackBlitz. The cost can be reproduced in any Chromium console, independent of Angular:

```js
const n = 500;
const atDepth = (depth, fn) => (depth === 0 ? fn() : atDepth(depth - 1, fn));
const measure = (depth, op) =>
  atDepth(depth, () => {
    const controllers = Array.from({ length: n }, () => new AbortController());
    const start = performance.now();
    for (const c of controllers) op(c);
    return `${(((performance.now() - start) / n) * 1000).toFixed(1)} µs`;
  });
for (const depth of [5, 100, 300]) {
  console.log(depth, 'abort():', measure(depth, (c) => c.abort()), 'abort(reason):', measure(depth, (c) => c.abort('destroyed')));
}
```

On the Angular side, it shows up whenever a view tree with many `resource()`/`rxResource()` instances is destroyed, e.g. on a route change.

### Please provide the exception or error you saw

```true
No error. Per destroyed resource, an AbortError DOMException including a stack trace is created.
```

### Please provide the environment you discovered this bug in (run `ng version`)

```true
Angular: 22.1.8 (the code above is unchanged on main e0260db)
Node: 24.18.0
Browser: Chromium 153 (Playwright 1.63)
```

